<p class="post-intro">World Agri-Tech Party – Day 2.</p>

<p>We spoke a lot about AI in Agriculture, and I have such dual feelings about it right now. Two days in London only amplified them.</p>

<h2>The first feeling: excitement</h2>

<p>The first feeling is obvious: excitement. AI is already bringing a lot of positive change to farming. This was confirmed at the expert panel on autonomous and precision farming and at a 40-minute workshop on Agentic AI in Agriculture.</p>

<p>The headliner of both was Dr Elliott Grant, who knows the subject not from the news, but from the depth &amp; inside.</p>

{{ photo(photos.watis2ElliottGrant) }}

<p>A few insightful things:</p>

<h3><span class="num">1.</span> A very dynamic market</h3>
<p>Around 400 ag automation startups are on the latest crop robotics landscape.</p>

<h3><span class="num">2.</span> The drivers are real</h3>
<p>I hear them on my own Zoom calls with farmers, especially in the Global South: labour shortages, rising input costs, pressure on pesticides.</p>

<h3><span class="num">3.</span> Quick AI wins are simple</h3>
<p>A robot that just moves fruit around a packhouse frees the best pickers to keep picking.</p>

<div class="photo-grid">
  {{ photo(photos.watis2RobLind, sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 410px") }}
  {{ photo(photos.watis2Spot, sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 410px") }}
</div>

<h3><span class="num">4.</span> Who owns the data?</h3>
<p>Farmers run mixed fleets of different brands, so interoperability and "who owns the data?" come up every time. From my own experience: in most cases your tech is co-existing with the tech zoo already deployed on the farm.</p>

<h3><span class="num">5.</span> Britain is still a bit British about it</h3>
<p>And on AI in UK agriculture: Britain is still a bit British about it (from the stage, not from me :)</p>

{{ photo(photos.watis2Panel) }}

{{ photo(photos.watis2BelindaClarke) }}

<h2>The second feeling: a healthy alarm</h2>

<p>So, the excited part is finished. Now the second, healthy feeling. Not fear, but inner alarm about the risks of AI.</p>

<p>You know that feeling when you meet a person with amazing depth of knowledge and unbelievable speed of work... but their emotional intelligence is still in its infancy? You don't know how to behave with that person...</p>

<p>Three scenarios if something goes wrong with AI (Geoffrey Hinton, the godfather of AI, said this, I'm just repeating):</p>
<ol class="post-steps">
  <li>Bad guys use AI on purpose &amp; create new global biological risks.</li>
  <li>Smart, good guys use it inaccurately and create something not in the plan (negligence; check the Hugging Face incident, brilliantly explained by Elliott 🤗).</li>
  <li>AI becomes smarter than us and decides it doesn't need humans anymore.</li>
</ol>

<div class="photo-grid">
  {{ photo(photos.watis2SlideAgenticEra, sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 410px") }}
  {{ photo(photos.watis2SlideAgenticReady, sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 410px") }}
</div>

<h2>So what to do? As usual, don't panic :)</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Be aware of the risks, but focus on your own universe, your best farm or agrifood production.</li>
  <li>Be aware of the risks, but keep moving forward with AI step by step.</li>
  <li>More people want AI for good: productivity, less routine, easier lives for growers.</li>
  <li>There is huge progress in good AI with vibe coding, 3D printing and GenAI, even in business pipelines.</li>
  <li>In the last month alone I've seen at least a dozen GitHub repositories where someone builds physical AI just for fun, but with obvious commercial potential. So, let's not be scared, but aware.</li>
  <li>You are not alone, we are all in this unclear time together.</li>
</ul>

